{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789266430,"nonce":"eUg8d5Td1qeSPAGw7M7EfRhVOT4=","title":"Cross-language references need proxies and handle rooting","message":"A Wren object held only by another language is invisible to wren_lift's\nown cycle, which is the only reclaimer of Wren objects: the other\nlanguage's reference does not appear among wren_lift's roots, so the\nobject is freed under it. In the other direction, a HaxeRef held only by\nWren cannot be represented at all yet, because Wren has no object kind\nfor a foreign reference; the protocol answers Type for it.\n\nThe design's answer is proxies plus handles. A Wren object crossing out\ngets a core Handle for as long as the foreign side holds it, and the\nforeign side sees a proxy that releases the handle when it dies (a\nHaxeRef-style wrapper on the Haxe side, an ObjForeign with a finalizer on\nthe Wren side). A Haxe object crossing into Wren becomes an ObjForeign\ncarrying the HaxeRef, answered by the same protocol. Both need the\nobject's lifetime tied to the proxy's, which is the finalizer path each\nruntime already has.\n\nUntil this lands, a value that crosses must not outlive the call that\ncarried it. The interop test respects that; real programs will not.","files":null}]}